        {
          "id": "PHOTO-05-C01",
          "document_id": "PHOTO-05",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 276,
          "text": "For basic night-sky photography, the NPS article lists a digital camera with manual controls, a wide-angle lens and a sturdy tripod. It recommends manual shooting mode. Long exposures collect light in dark scenes. This is general advice, not a Canon EOS R1-specific procedure.",
          "sections": [
            "Equipment List; Set Up (manual mode)"
          ],
          "tokens_with_specials": 63
        },
        {
          "id": "PHOTO-05-C02",
          "document_id": "PHOTO-05",
          "start": 278,
          "end": 731,
          "text": "NPS suggests opening the lens as wide as it allows, mentioning f/1.4 to f/3.5; beginning at ISO 400 and increasing as needed; and experimenting with exposures of 10 to 20 seconds. These are experimental starting settings in the article's tripod-based night-sky context, not guaranteed settings for every lens or a Canon EOS R1. Longer exposures can produce star trails as Earth rotates. The article does not provide an R1-specific maximum exposure time.",
          "sections": [
            "Set Up (suggested settings)"
          ],
          "tokens_with_specials": 106
        },
        {
          "id": "PHOTO-05-C03",
          "document_id": "PHOTO-05",
          "start": 733,
          "end": 1103,
          "text": "NPS suggests automatic white balance and a two-to-five-second self-timer, a remote release, or a compatible smartphone. With camera and tripod positioned, manually focus using a distant bright light, such as a star, streetlight or house. These are general techniques; the article does not identify Canon EOS R1 menu paths or prove compatibility with a particular remote.",
          "sections": [
            "Set Up (white balance, release and focus)"
          ],
          "tokens_with_specials": 80
        },
        {
          "id": "PHOTO-06-C01",
          "document_id": "PHOTO-06",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 164,
          "text": "The NPS beginner guide suggests using the rule of thirds, surrounding objects as a frame, and leading lines to draw attention to the subject in outdoor photographs.",
          "sections": [
            "Composition"
          ],
          "tokens_with_specials": 34
        },
        {
          "id": "PHOTO-06-C02",
          "document_id": "PHOTO-06",
          "start": 166,
          "end": 345,
          "text": "For a smoother moving-water effect, the guide suggests a slower shutter speed and keeping the camera steady, preferably on a tripod. It gives no universal waterfall exposure time.",
          "sections": [
            "Adjust Your Settings — Shutter speed"
          ],
          "tokens_with_specials": 39
        },
        {
          "id": "PHOTO-06-C03",
          "document_id": "PHOTO-06",
          "start": 347,
          "end": 616,
          "text": "The guide explains that a larger lens opening has a lower f-number, admits more light and gives shallower depth of field. A higher f-number can make the background clearer. These are general relationships, not guaranteed landscape sharpness or an EOS R1 setting recipe.",
          "sections": [
            "Adjust Your Settings — Aperture"
          ],
          "tokens_with_specials": 58
        },
        {
          "id": "PHOTO-06-C04",
          "document_id": "PHOTO-06",
          "start": 618,
          "end": 751,
          "text": "The guide describes shortly after sunrise or before sunset as golden hour, when sunlight has a warmer appearance and softer lighting.",
          "sections": [
            "When to Shoot — Golden Hour"
          ],
          "tokens_with_specials": 25
        }
      ]
    },
